1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,140
Meet Maya.

2
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:04,388
She trains artificial intelligence in a

3
00:00:04,388 --> 00:00:05,224
lab in Montreal,

4
00:00:05,224 --> 00:00:06,589
and this morning her phone won't stop

5
00:00:06,589 --> 00:00:07,230
buzzing.

6
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,697
Headline after headline says the same

7
00:00:08,697 --> 00:00:09,460
thing.

8
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:12,530
Canada's economy is crashing.

9
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,880
Then a message from California.

10
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,840
A big AI lab wants her.

11
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,218
Bigger paycheck,

12
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:19,877
bigger computers,

13
00:00:19,877 --> 00:00:20,940
more sunshine.

14
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:22,526
Pack your bags,

15
00:00:22,526 --> 00:00:23,940
it says.

16
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,056
Meanwhile,

17
00:00:25,056 --> 00:00:26,081
far to the south,

18
00:00:26,081 --> 00:00:27,107
in the desert,

19
00:00:27,107 --> 00:00:29,520
somebody else is packing too.

20
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:30,870
This is Whirr.

21
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:32,461
Whirr is a data center,

22
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:34,340
and Whirr is overheating.

23
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,696
Its fans are screaming,

24
00:00:35,696 --> 00:00:37,511
its power bill keeps climbing,

25
00:00:37,511 --> 00:00:39,440
and it needs a new home.

26
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:41,700
Maya is thinking of moving south.

27
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,880
Whirr is thinking of moving north.

28
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,350
One of them has read the map

29
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:45,920
wrong.

30
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,240
So which is it?

31
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,314
Is Canada losing the AI boom,

32
00:00:49,314 --> 00:00:51,199
or could it be the biggest winner

33
00:00:51,199 --> 00:00:52,133
of all?

34
00:00:52,583 --> 00:00:59,500
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

35
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:16,263
To be fair,

36
00:01:16,263 --> 00:01:18,260
the bad news is real.

37
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:20,576
Back in the spring of twenty twenty-five,

38
00:01:20,576 --> 00:01:23,126
American tariffs hit Canadian cars and

39
00:01:23,126 --> 00:01:24,167
machinery,

40
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:24,995
exports fell,

41
00:01:24,995 --> 00:01:27,670
and the economy actually shrank.

42
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:30,000
Maya's uncle sells car parts.

43
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,180
He stopped smiling at the news.

44
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:34,120
The headlines did the math.

45
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,346
Crash.

46
00:01:35,584 --> 00:01:38,554
But crashes don't usually bounce.

47
00:01:38,704 --> 00:01:41,225
This year the economy grew again,

48
00:01:41,225 --> 00:01:43,537
and the Bank of Canada expects more

49
00:01:43,537 --> 00:01:44,439
growth ahead,

50
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:46,520
even if new tariffs slow things down

51
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:47,814
this winter.

52
00:01:48,234 --> 00:01:49,444
That's a forecast,

53
00:01:49,444 --> 00:01:50,804
not a promise.

54
00:01:50,804 --> 00:01:51,368
Still,

55
00:01:51,368 --> 00:01:54,344
it's not the picture on Maya's phone.

56
00:01:54,584 --> 00:01:56,502
And while everyone stared at the

57
00:01:56,502 --> 00:01:57,250
headlines,

58
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:59,364
something else was happening.

59
00:01:59,364 --> 00:02:02,104
The AI boom went shopping.

60
00:02:02,154 --> 00:02:04,015
And its shopping list looks a lot

61
00:02:04,015 --> 00:02:06,244
like a map of Canada.

62
00:02:06,484 --> 00:02:09,229
Let me draw what an AI factory

63
00:02:09,229 --> 00:02:10,104
needs.

64
00:02:10,104 --> 00:02:11,485
Start with a plug:

65
00:02:11,485 --> 00:02:14,116
huge amounts of electricity,

66
00:02:14,116 --> 00:02:16,014
all day and all night.

67
00:02:16,014 --> 00:02:17,007
Then a fridge:

68
00:02:17,007 --> 00:02:19,585
every chip turns power into heat,

69
00:02:19,585 --> 00:02:22,314
and the heat has to go somewhere.

70
00:02:22,364 --> 00:02:23,327
Then a mine:

71
00:02:23,327 --> 00:02:23,762
chips,

72
00:02:23,762 --> 00:02:26,884
cables and batteries are made of metal.

73
00:02:26,964 --> 00:02:28,147
And finally,

74
00:02:28,147 --> 00:02:28,920
a brain:

75
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:30,851
people who know how to build the

76
00:02:30,851 --> 00:02:31,934
models.

77
00:02:31,984 --> 00:02:34,046
Most countries are strong in a couple

78
00:02:34,046 --> 00:02:35,064
of these.

79
00:02:35,064 --> 00:02:37,899
Very few have the whole list.

80
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:43,721
Whirr heads north first,

81
00:02:43,721 --> 00:02:46,520
following the power lines.

82
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:48,574
They lead out of the mountains and

83
00:02:48,574 --> 00:02:49,833
down the rivers,

84
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:52,230
to dams the size of small towns.

85
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:54,154
Of all its electricity,

86
00:02:54,154 --> 00:02:56,775
Canada makes about six of every ten

87
00:02:56,775 --> 00:02:59,190
kilowatt hours from falling water.

88
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:02,085
Whirr has never seen anything like it.

89
00:03:02,350 --> 00:03:04,825
That makes Canada the third-biggest

90
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:07,900
hydropower maker on the planet.

91
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:10,100
Whirr does a little dance.

92
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:10,662
Clean,

93
00:03:10,662 --> 00:03:11,520
steady,

94
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:13,599
and already built.

95
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:14,842
In Quebec,

96
00:03:14,842 --> 00:03:17,730
the power is famous for being cheap.

97
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,705
A big factory there pays less than

98
00:03:19,705 --> 00:03:21,822
a quarter of what the same factory

99
00:03:21,822 --> 00:03:23,480
would pay in Boston.

100
00:03:23,810 --> 00:03:25,561
We told that story in Could Cheap

101
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:28,350
Electricity Become the New Oil?

102
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:30,750
Whirr starts unpacking.

103
00:03:31,017 --> 00:03:33,147
Then comes the twist.

104
00:03:33,317 --> 00:03:35,690
Hydro-Québec says its days of surplus

105
00:03:35,690 --> 00:03:37,477
power are over.

106
00:03:37,737 --> 00:03:39,960
Big new users now need the government's

107
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:40,827
permission,

108
00:03:40,827 --> 00:03:42,828
and the price it has proposed for

109
00:03:42,828 --> 00:03:45,051
data centers is more than double what

110
00:03:45,051 --> 00:03:47,207
big industry pays today.

111
00:03:47,357 --> 00:03:48,795
It's still a proposal,

112
00:03:48,795 --> 00:03:50,437
still under review.

113
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:52,785
Whirr stops unpacking.

114
00:03:53,050 --> 00:03:53,804
Next door,

115
00:03:53,804 --> 00:03:55,599
in British Columbia,

116
00:03:55,599 --> 00:03:58,199
big AI and data center projects no

117
00:03:58,199 --> 00:04:01,400
longer simply join the line for power.

118
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:03,400
They now have to compete for a

119
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:04,521
limited slice,

120
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:05,871
like a talent show,

121
00:04:05,871 --> 00:04:07,701
judged on what they give back to

122
00:04:07,701 --> 00:04:08,987
the province.

123
00:04:09,250 --> 00:04:11,322
So Whirr learns the first lesson of

124
00:04:11,322 --> 00:04:12,370
the north.

125
00:04:12,370 --> 00:04:14,080
Canada has the power.

126
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:14,836
But the cheap,

127
00:04:14,836 --> 00:04:17,570
easy power is already spoken for.

128
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,032
The next power has to be built,

129
00:04:20,032 --> 00:04:22,991
and dams and power lines take years.

130
00:04:27,450 --> 00:04:27,970
Still,

131
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:30,433
the north has one gift nobody has

132
00:04:30,433 --> 00:04:31,480
to build.

133
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:32,768
Whirr steps outside,

134
00:04:32,768 --> 00:04:35,108
and for the first time in ages,

135
00:04:35,108 --> 00:04:36,760
its fans slow down.

136
00:04:36,810 --> 00:04:37,957
In the desert,

137
00:04:37,957 --> 00:04:40,299
a data center spends a fortune on

138
00:04:40,299 --> 00:04:41,940
air conditioning.

139
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:42,420
Up here,

140
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:43,578
the cold air does a lot of

141
00:04:43,578 --> 00:04:44,820
that work for free.

142
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:46,180
Whirr sighs.

143
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:47,720
Ahhh.

144
00:04:47,984 --> 00:04:50,734
One Quebec operator says it can cool

145
00:04:50,734 --> 00:04:52,984
its servers with outside air most of

146
00:04:52,984 --> 00:04:54,004
the year.

147
00:04:54,004 --> 00:04:55,072
That's its own claim,

148
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:57,032
and it depends on how a building

149
00:04:57,032 --> 00:04:58,504
is designed.

150
00:04:58,654 --> 00:05:00,504
But the idea is simple.

151
00:05:00,504 --> 00:05:02,124
The cold is a giant,

152
00:05:02,124 --> 00:05:03,822
natural fridge.

153
00:05:04,084 --> 00:05:06,136
And the heat that comes out of

154
00:05:06,136 --> 00:05:09,044
the servers doesn't have to be wasted.

155
00:05:09,254 --> 00:05:09,976
In Kamloops,

156
00:05:09,976 --> 00:05:11,067
British Columbia,

157
00:05:11,067 --> 00:05:12,733
the phone company Bell plans an AI

158
00:05:12,733 --> 00:05:15,138
data center at a university that will

159
00:05:15,138 --> 00:05:17,174
pipe its leftover heat into the campus

160
00:05:17,174 --> 00:05:17,904
buildings.

161
00:05:17,904 --> 00:05:19,926
The servers keep the students warm.

162
00:05:20,184 --> 00:05:22,324
The giants have noticed.

163
00:05:22,384 --> 00:05:25,162
Microsoft has announced plans worth almost

164
00:05:25,162 --> 00:05:28,444
fourteen billion US dollars in Canada over

165
00:05:28,444 --> 00:05:29,429
a few years,

166
00:05:29,429 --> 00:05:32,554
mostly for cloud and AI data centers.

167
00:05:32,804 --> 00:05:34,318
That's a company plan,

168
00:05:34,318 --> 00:05:36,454
not money already spent.

169
00:05:36,454 --> 00:05:38,451
But it's a big bet on the

170
00:05:38,451 --> 00:05:39,337
north.

171
00:05:39,584 --> 00:05:41,516
Let me draw why the cold is

172
00:05:41,516 --> 00:05:42,944
worth money.

173
00:05:43,004 --> 00:05:44,384
Here's a server.

174
00:05:44,414 --> 00:05:45,496
Power goes in,

175
00:05:45,496 --> 00:05:47,482
and almost all of it comes out

176
00:05:47,482 --> 00:05:48,524
as heat.

177
00:05:48,714 --> 00:05:50,029
In a hot place,

178
00:05:50,029 --> 00:05:51,156
you pay twice:

179
00:05:51,156 --> 00:05:52,739
once to run the chip,

180
00:05:52,739 --> 00:05:55,754
and again to pump the heat away.

181
00:05:55,934 --> 00:05:57,286
In a cold place,

182
00:05:57,286 --> 00:05:59,769
the outside air carries much of the

183
00:05:59,769 --> 00:06:02,024
heat off almost for free.

184
00:06:02,134 --> 00:06:03,443
And if you're lucky,

185
00:06:03,443 --> 00:06:05,374
a neighbor wants that heat.

186
00:06:05,384 --> 00:06:06,350
Same chip,

187
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:07,950
smaller bill.

188
00:06:08,184 --> 00:06:10,474
Whirr makes up its mind.

189
00:06:10,474 --> 00:06:11,176
Cold air,

190
00:06:11,176 --> 00:06:12,121
clean power,

191
00:06:12,121 --> 00:06:14,144
and neighbors who want it.

192
00:06:14,144 --> 00:06:16,471
It starts looking for a lot.

193
00:06:20,917 --> 00:06:21,863
Meanwhile,

194
00:06:21,863 --> 00:06:23,780
Maya goes home for the weekend,

195
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:24,968
to Sudbury,

196
00:06:24,968 --> 00:06:25,914
Ontario,

197
00:06:25,914 --> 00:06:26,861
where her dad,

198
00:06:26,861 --> 00:06:27,321
Luc,

199
00:06:27,321 --> 00:06:29,377
works at a nickel mine.

200
00:06:29,437 --> 00:06:31,477
He has heard about California.

201
00:06:31,657 --> 00:06:34,127
You know what's inside those AI computers?

202
00:06:34,127 --> 00:06:35,287
he asks.

203
00:06:35,347 --> 00:06:36,277
Rocks.

204
00:06:36,277 --> 00:06:37,706
Our rocks.

205
00:06:37,950 --> 00:06:39,920
He's not wrong.

206
00:06:39,930 --> 00:06:41,540
Copper for the wires.

207
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:42,064
Nickel,

208
00:06:42,064 --> 00:06:44,420
lithium and graphite for the batteries.

209
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,361
Uranium for the nuclear plants that could

210
00:06:46,361 --> 00:06:48,320
power the data centers.

211
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:50,466
Canada's government keeps an official list

212
00:06:50,466 --> 00:06:52,714
of minerals the modern economy can't do

213
00:06:52,714 --> 00:06:53,660
without.

214
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:55,279
There are thirty-four on it,

215
00:06:55,279 --> 00:06:57,853
and Canada mines many of them.

216
00:06:58,117 --> 00:06:59,212
In uranium,

217
00:06:59,212 --> 00:07:01,447
Canada is the world's second-biggest

218
00:07:01,447 --> 00:07:02,717
producer.

219
00:07:02,737 --> 00:07:04,117
Luc grins.

220
00:07:04,117 --> 00:07:04,560
Rocks,

221
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:06,093
he says again.

222
00:07:06,350 --> 00:07:09,070
But here's the catch with rocks.

223
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:10,650
They're slow.

224
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:12,109
Around the world,

225
00:07:12,109 --> 00:07:13,936
a big new mine has taken more

226
00:07:13,936 --> 00:07:15,054
than sixteen years,

227
00:07:15,054 --> 00:07:16,173
on average,

228
00:07:16,173 --> 00:07:18,457
to go from discovery to the first

229
00:07:18,457 --> 00:07:19,970
truckload of ore.

230
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:21,157
By then,

231
00:07:21,157 --> 00:07:24,817
Maya's AI models would be ancient history.

232
00:07:25,084 --> 00:07:27,579
Ottawa has set up a Major Projects

233
00:07:27,579 --> 00:07:29,994
Office to speed up approvals.

234
00:07:30,494 --> 00:07:32,884
One copper and zinc mine in Saskatchewan

235
00:07:32,884 --> 00:07:35,492
was referred to it and started producing

236
00:07:35,492 --> 00:07:36,991
less than a year later,

237
00:07:36,991 --> 00:07:39,764
although it was already mostly built.

238
00:07:39,954 --> 00:07:41,704
Fast for a mine.

239
00:07:42,094 --> 00:07:44,972
Whether that becomes normal is the real

240
00:07:44,972 --> 00:07:45,833
test.

241
00:07:50,284 --> 00:07:51,434
Back in Montreal,

242
00:07:51,434 --> 00:07:53,053
Maya walks into her lab,

243
00:07:53,053 --> 00:07:55,399
and you can see why California wants

244
00:07:55,399 --> 00:07:56,224
her.

245
00:07:56,224 --> 00:07:58,369
Some of the big ideas behind today's

246
00:07:58,369 --> 00:07:59,634
AI grew up right here,

247
00:07:59,634 --> 00:08:01,757
in Canadian universities,

248
00:08:01,757 --> 00:08:03,473
when most of the world had given

249
00:08:03,473 --> 00:08:04,716
up on them.

250
00:08:04,950 --> 00:08:06,042
In Toronto,

251
00:08:06,042 --> 00:08:08,047
Geoffrey Hinton spent decades on

252
00:08:08,047 --> 00:08:10,029
artificial neural networks,

253
00:08:10,029 --> 00:08:11,589
when much of the field thought they

254
00:08:11,589 --> 00:08:13,080
were a dead end.

255
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:14,949
In twenty twenty-four,

256
00:08:14,949 --> 00:08:17,179
that work helped earn him the Nobel

257
00:08:17,179 --> 00:08:18,770
Prize in Physics.

258
00:08:19,017 --> 00:08:20,022
In Montreal,

259
00:08:20,022 --> 00:08:22,598
Yoshua Bengio founded the lab that grew

260
00:08:22,598 --> 00:08:23,861
into Mila,

261
00:08:23,861 --> 00:08:26,180
which describes itself as the world's

262
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:29,272
largest academic research center for deep

263
00:08:29,272 --> 00:08:30,387
learning.

264
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:33,425
Maya's lab is just down the street.

265
00:08:33,684 --> 00:08:36,161
Canada was also the first country in

266
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:38,188
the world to launch a national AI

267
00:08:38,188 --> 00:08:39,464
strategy.

268
00:08:39,574 --> 00:08:41,330
And now it's paying to build a

269
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:44,063
giant AI supercomputer of its own,

270
00:08:44,063 --> 00:08:46,168
owned in Canada.

271
00:08:46,417 --> 00:08:48,757
So where's the problem?

272
00:08:49,007 --> 00:08:51,067
Follow Maya's classmates.

273
00:08:51,067 --> 00:08:52,914
Most science and tech graduates do stay

274
00:08:52,914 --> 00:08:53,920
in Canada,

275
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:55,957
Statistics Canada finds.

276
00:08:56,097 --> 00:08:58,245
But the stars get the biggest offers,

277
00:08:58,245 --> 00:09:00,631
and many of those come from American

278
00:09:00,631 --> 00:09:02,967
companies with deeper pockets.

279
00:09:03,147 --> 00:09:05,638
Promising Canadian startups often find

280
00:09:05,638 --> 00:09:06,748
their biggest buyers,

281
00:09:06,748 --> 00:09:08,085
and their biggest checks,

282
00:09:08,085 --> 00:09:09,817
south of the border.

283
00:09:09,927 --> 00:09:11,747
Maya's phone buzzes.

284
00:09:11,747 --> 00:09:13,317
The recruiter again.

285
00:09:13,317 --> 00:09:14,687
Decided yet?

286
00:09:14,737 --> 00:09:16,075
Not yet.

287
00:09:16,317 --> 00:09:19,427
Let me draw Canada's talent pipeline.

288
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:20,662
Students go in here,

289
00:09:20,662 --> 00:09:22,587
at the universities.

290
00:09:22,587 --> 00:09:24,412
They come out as researchers,

291
00:09:24,412 --> 00:09:26,167
and then as founders.

292
00:09:26,217 --> 00:09:27,657
But look at this pipe.

293
00:09:27,657 --> 00:09:28,819
There's a leak,

294
00:09:28,819 --> 00:09:30,053
and it points south:

295
00:09:30,053 --> 00:09:30,978
higher pay,

296
00:09:30,978 --> 00:09:32,378
bigger computers,

297
00:09:32,378 --> 00:09:33,867
bigger funding.

298
00:09:33,867 --> 00:09:36,307
Canada doesn't need to stop the water.

299
00:09:36,307 --> 00:09:38,059
It needs to give it reasons to

300
00:09:38,059 --> 00:09:38,541
stay:

301
00:09:38,541 --> 00:09:40,490
computers to work on at home,

302
00:09:40,490 --> 00:09:42,680
and companies big enough to grow up

303
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:43,499
here.

304
00:09:47,950 --> 00:09:50,238
Which brings us to the biggest catch

305
00:09:50,238 --> 00:09:51,410
of all.

306
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:54,784
Canada's best customer is also its biggest

307
00:09:54,784 --> 00:09:55,630
risk.

308
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:58,324
Of every dollar of goods Canada sells

309
00:09:58,324 --> 00:09:58,954
abroad,

310
00:09:58,954 --> 00:10:01,341
nearly three of every four dollars go

311
00:10:01,341 --> 00:10:03,460
to the United States.

312
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,806
When Washington adds tariffs,

313
00:10:05,806 --> 00:10:08,157
Canada feels it first.

314
00:10:08,417 --> 00:10:11,007
AI could change that list.

315
00:10:11,007 --> 00:10:13,989
Electricity turned into computing doesn't

316
00:10:13,989 --> 00:10:15,237
ride on trucks.

317
00:10:15,237 --> 00:10:17,387
It travels through cables.

318
00:10:17,487 --> 00:10:19,134
That could be a new kind of

319
00:10:19,134 --> 00:10:19,817
export,

320
00:10:19,817 --> 00:10:22,405
but only if Canada builds the data

321
00:10:22,405 --> 00:10:23,088
centers,

322
00:10:23,088 --> 00:10:25,206
and only if someone at home owns

323
00:10:25,206 --> 00:10:27,453
the companies that use them.

324
00:10:27,717 --> 00:10:29,887
So here's the scorecard.

325
00:10:29,887 --> 00:10:30,723
Power:

326
00:10:30,723 --> 00:10:31,481
plenty,

327
00:10:31,481 --> 00:10:33,546
but the cheap part is taken,

328
00:10:33,546 --> 00:10:36,237
and new dams and lines take years.

329
00:10:36,297 --> 00:10:37,115
Cold:

330
00:10:37,115 --> 00:10:38,087
free.

331
00:10:38,157 --> 00:10:38,916
Metals:

332
00:10:38,916 --> 00:10:39,841
in the ground,

333
00:10:39,841 --> 00:10:41,627
but slow to dig up.

334
00:10:41,637 --> 00:10:42,493
Brains:

335
00:10:42,493 --> 00:10:43,621
world class,

336
00:10:43,621 --> 00:10:45,777
but tempted south.

337
00:10:45,927 --> 00:10:46,955
And next door,

338
00:10:46,955 --> 00:10:49,854
a giant neighbor that can slow everything

339
00:10:49,854 --> 00:10:51,772
down with a tariff.

340
00:10:56,217 --> 00:10:56,689
So,

341
00:10:56,689 --> 00:10:59,422
could Canada be the biggest winner of

342
00:10:59,422 --> 00:11:00,767
the AI boom?

343
00:11:00,897 --> 00:11:02,524
It has something rare:

344
00:11:02,524 --> 00:11:04,083
the whole shopping list,

345
00:11:04,083 --> 00:11:05,587
in one country.

346
00:11:05,657 --> 00:11:06,537
Power,

347
00:11:06,537 --> 00:11:07,113
cold,

348
00:11:07,113 --> 00:11:08,927
metals and brains.

349
00:11:08,947 --> 00:11:10,698
Most countries would trade a lot for

350
00:11:10,698 --> 00:11:11,517
that.

351
00:11:11,617 --> 00:11:13,486
But having the list isn't the same

352
00:11:13,486 --> 00:11:14,787
as winning.

353
00:11:14,847 --> 00:11:17,120
Canada wins only if it builds:

354
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:18,212
power lines,

355
00:11:18,212 --> 00:11:18,858
mines,

356
00:11:18,858 --> 00:11:19,950
data centers,

357
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:21,956
and companies that grow up at home

358
00:11:21,956 --> 00:11:24,116
instead of moving away.

359
00:11:24,350 --> 00:11:27,090
And that crash everyone talks about?

360
00:11:27,150 --> 00:11:29,018
It's real for some industries,

361
00:11:29,018 --> 00:11:31,510
like the car parts hit by tariffs.

362
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:33,224
But the AI boom is betting on

363
00:11:33,224 --> 00:11:35,140
a different Canada.

364
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:37,019
The one behind the dams,

365
00:11:37,019 --> 00:11:38,339
under the snow,

366
00:11:38,339 --> 00:11:40,286
and inside the labs.

367
00:11:40,550 --> 00:11:41,853
Back in Montreal,

368
00:11:41,853 --> 00:11:45,044
Maya opens the California offer one more

369
00:11:45,044 --> 00:11:45,910
time.

370
00:11:45,940 --> 00:11:48,080
Then she looks out the window.

371
00:11:48,210 --> 00:11:49,295
Across the street,

372
00:11:49,295 --> 00:11:51,044
a new building is going up,

373
00:11:51,044 --> 00:11:53,479
and a friendly data center waves at

374
00:11:53,479 --> 00:11:54,300
her.

375
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:55,450
It's Whirr.

376
00:11:55,450 --> 00:11:56,295
I moved in,

377
00:11:56,295 --> 00:11:57,080
it says.

378
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:58,400
Your turn.

379
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:00,330
Maya types her answer.

380
00:12:00,470 --> 00:12:02,400
Ask me again next year.

381
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:03,450
For now,

382
00:12:03,450 --> 00:12:04,774
I'm staying.

383
00:12:05,017 --> 00:12:05,624
Next time,

384
00:12:05,624 --> 00:12:08,343
we follow the money behind another hidden

385
00:12:08,343 --> 00:12:10,617
link of the world economy.

386
00:12:10,797 --> 00:12:12,871
This is The Hidden Economics.
